    <h1>John Quincy Adams</h1>
    <div class="meta">
        <strong>Party:</strong> Whig (formerly Democratic-Republican)<br>
        <strong>Term:</strong> 1825 &ndash; 1829<br>
        <strong>Vice President:</strong> John C. Calhoun<br>
        <strong>Birth:</strong> July 11, 1767<br>
        <strong>Death:</strong> February 23, 1848
    </div>

    <h2>Fun Fact</h2>
    <p>
        Adams was the last president who had personally
        been part of the Revolutionary War era. He spoke
        so many languages that he often conducted diplomatic
        conversations in them. At age 10, he accompanied
        his father John Adams on a diplomatic mission to
        France, where he learned French from the French
        foreign minister. He could carry on entire
        conversations in 12 languages including
        Dutch, French, German, Italian, Latin, and
        Spanish.
    </p>
    <p>
        In a remarkably unusual twist, Adams served in
        Congress for 17 years as a Representative from
        Massachusetts <em>after</em> his presidency,
        where he argued a famous case before the Supreme Court
        defending the rights of enslaved Africans who had
        mutinied aboard the Spanish ship <em>Amistad</em>.
    </p>

